1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,354
Scripts fail.

2
00:00:01,354 --> 00:00:02,404
A disk fills up,

3
00:00:02,404 --> 00:00:03,586
a file is missing,

4
00:00:03,586 --> 00:00:05,228
a server does not answer.

5
00:00:05,228 --> 00:00:08,117
The question is whether your script notices,

6
00:00:08,117 --> 00:00:08,970
stops safely,

7
00:00:08,970 --> 00:00:11,465
cleans up and tells you what happened.

8
00:00:12,015 --> 00:00:13,919
Today you learn four things.

9
00:00:13,919 --> 00:00:14,667
Exit codes.

10
00:00:14,667 --> 00:00:15,482
Strict mode,

11
00:00:15,482 --> 00:00:17,454
to stop on the first failure.

12
00:00:17,454 --> 00:00:17,862
Traps,

13
00:00:17,862 --> 00:00:18,677
to clean up.

14
00:00:18,677 --> 00:00:19,493
And logging,

15
00:00:19,493 --> 00:00:21,125
to leave a clear record.

16
00:00:21,675 --> 00:00:24,469
Every demo is a real recording on Ubuntu.

17
00:00:26,519 --> 00:00:26,960
First,

18
00:00:26,960 --> 00:00:27,767
exit codes.

19
00:00:27,767 --> 00:00:30,190
Every command ends with a number.

20
00:00:30,190 --> 00:00:31,585
Zero means success,

21
00:00:31,585 --> 00:00:33,935
and anything else means failure.

22
00:00:33,935 --> 00:00:35,770
Run a command that works.

23
00:00:35,770 --> 00:00:36,505
Type: L S,

24
00:00:36,505 --> 00:00:38,487
slash E T C slash hostname.

25
00:00:39,037 --> 00:00:40,710
It printed the file name.

26
00:00:40,710 --> 00:00:42,451
Now ask for its exit code,

27
00:00:42,451 --> 00:00:44,191
with dollar question mark.

28
00:00:44,191 --> 00:00:44,927
Type: echo,

29
00:00:44,927 --> 00:00:46,333
dollar question mark.

30
00:00:46,883 --> 00:00:51,512
Zero, success. Now a command that fails. Type: L S, slash nope.

31
00:00:52,062 --> 00:00:57,374
An error message. Ask for the exit code again. Type: echo, dollar question mark.

32
00:00:57,924 --> 00:00:58,196
Two.

33
00:00:58,196 --> 00:00:58,808
Not zero,

34
00:00:58,808 --> 00:01:00,305
so the command failed.

35
00:01:00,305 --> 00:01:02,957
Your own scripts can return a code too,

36
00:01:02,957 --> 00:01:04,453
with the exit command.

37
00:01:04,453 --> 00:01:06,425
Here is a small check script.

38
00:01:06,425 --> 00:01:08,058
Type: cat check dot S H.

39
00:01:08,608 --> 00:01:11,048
If the log contains the word ERROR,

40
00:01:11,048 --> 00:01:14,326
the script prints a message and exits with one.

41
00:01:14,326 --> 00:01:16,139
Otherwise it prints clean,

42
00:01:16,139 --> 00:01:17,534
and exits with zero.

43
00:01:17,534 --> 00:01:18,022
Run it.

44
00:01:18,022 --> 00:01:19,765
Type: bash check dot S H.

45
00:01:20,315 --> 00:01:24,582
It found errors. Check the code. Type: echo, dollar question mark.

46
00:01:25,132 --> 00:01:25,388
One.

47
00:01:25,388 --> 00:01:26,156
Other tools,

48
00:01:26,156 --> 00:01:28,076
like pipelines and schedulers,

49
00:01:28,076 --> 00:01:29,164
read this number,

50
00:01:29,164 --> 00:01:30,828
to decide what to do next.

51
00:01:32,878 --> 00:01:33,226
Next,

52
00:01:33,226 --> 00:01:34,062
strict mode.

53
00:01:34,062 --> 00:01:34,828
By default,

54
00:01:34,828 --> 00:01:36,988
bash carries on after an error,

55
00:01:36,988 --> 00:01:38,799
and that can be dangerous.

56
00:01:38,799 --> 00:01:40,401
Here is a loose script.

57
00:01:40,401 --> 00:01:42,073
Type: cat loose dot S H.

58
00:01:42,623 --> 00:01:46,295
It tries to change into a folder that does not exist,

59
00:01:46,295 --> 00:01:47,680
and then carries on.

60
00:01:47,680 --> 00:01:48,165
Run it.

61
00:01:48,165 --> 00:01:49,897
Type: bash loose dot S H.

62
00:01:50,447 --> 00:01:51,435
The cd failed,

63
00:01:51,435 --> 00:01:53,057
but the script went on.

64
00:01:53,057 --> 00:01:56,654
It reported a folder that is not the one it wanted,

65
00:01:56,654 --> 00:01:59,405
and used a variable that was never set.

66
00:01:59,405 --> 00:02:00,887
Now a strict version.

67
00:02:00,887 --> 00:02:02,650
Type: cat strict dot S H.

68
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:05,419
The set line has three options.

69
00:02:05,419 --> 00:02:08,427
Dash E stops at the first failing command.

70
00:02:08,427 --> 00:02:11,577
Dash U treats an unset variable as an error.

71
00:02:11,577 --> 00:02:15,515
And pipe fail makes a pipeline fail if any stage fails.

72
00:02:15,515 --> 00:02:16,016
Run it.

73
00:02:16,016 --> 00:02:17,877
Type: bash strict dot S H.

74
00:02:18,427 --> 00:02:20,299
It stopped at the failed cd,

75
00:02:20,299 --> 00:02:21,970
and nothing after it ran.

76
00:02:21,970 --> 00:02:23,307
Check the exit code.

77
00:02:23,307 --> 00:02:24,042
Type: echo,

78
00:02:24,042 --> 00:02:25,446
dollar question mark.

79
00:02:25,996 --> 00:02:26,274
One.

80
00:02:26,274 --> 00:02:28,015
Now see what dash U does.

81
00:02:28,015 --> 00:02:31,984
Here is a script that uses a variable that was never set.

82
00:02:31,984 --> 00:02:33,655
Type: cat unset dot S H.

83
00:02:34,205 --> 00:02:36,893
Run it. Type: bash unset dot S H.

84
00:02:37,443 --> 00:02:38,469
Unbound variable.

85
00:02:38,469 --> 00:02:39,374
Without dash U,

86
00:02:39,374 --> 00:02:42,030
the script would have printed an empty name,

87
00:02:42,030 --> 00:02:42,755
and gone on.

88
00:02:44,805 --> 00:02:46,305
Stopping early is good,

89
00:02:46,305 --> 00:02:48,978
but a script that stops can leave a mess.

90
00:02:48,978 --> 00:02:51,912
A trap runs a command when something happens.

91
00:02:51,912 --> 00:02:54,911
Here is a script with a trap on the word EXIT.

92
00:02:54,911 --> 00:02:56,410
Type: cat trap dot S H.

93
00:02:56,960 --> 00:02:58,807
It makes a temporary file,

94
00:02:58,807 --> 00:03:02,996
and sets a trap that removes the file and prints a message.

95
00:03:02,996 --> 00:03:04,487
Then a command fails.

96
00:03:04,487 --> 00:03:04,984
Run it.

97
00:03:04,984 --> 00:03:06,688
Type: bash trap dot S H.

98
00:03:07,238 --> 00:03:09,381
The script stopped at the failure,

99
00:03:09,381 --> 00:03:10,831
and the trap still ran.

100
00:03:10,831 --> 00:03:12,091
Check the exit code.

101
00:03:12,091 --> 00:03:12,784
Type: echo,

102
00:03:12,784 --> 00:03:14,108
dollar question mark.

103
00:03:14,658 --> 00:03:14,938
One,

104
00:03:14,938 --> 00:03:16,060
still a failure.

105
00:03:16,060 --> 00:03:19,777
Now check that nothing was left behind in slash temp.

106
00:03:19,777 --> 00:03:20,478
Type: L S,

107
00:03:20,478 --> 00:03:21,250
slash temp.

108
00:03:21,800 --> 00:03:22,187
Empty.

109
00:03:22,187 --> 00:03:25,737
The trap on EXIT runs however the script ends: success,

110
00:03:25,737 --> 00:03:27,286
failure or an interrupt.

111
00:03:27,286 --> 00:03:29,352
That is how you clean up safely.

112
00:03:31,402 --> 00:03:31,767
Next,

113
00:03:31,767 --> 00:03:32,351
logging.

114
00:03:32,351 --> 00:03:34,979
A good script says what it is doing,

115
00:03:34,979 --> 00:03:35,636
and when.

116
00:03:35,636 --> 00:03:37,753
Here is a small log function.

117
00:03:37,753 --> 00:03:39,359
Type: cat log dot S H.

118
00:03:39,909 --> 00:03:41,886
The function prints the time,

119
00:03:41,886 --> 00:03:43,385
a level and a message,

120
00:03:43,385 --> 00:03:44,612
to standard error,

121
00:03:44,612 --> 00:03:45,838
with the redirect,

122
00:03:45,838 --> 00:03:47,678
greater than ampersand two.

123
00:03:47,678 --> 00:03:48,155
Run it.

124
00:03:48,155 --> 00:03:49,723
Type: bash log dot S H.

125
00:03:50,273 --> 00:03:51,592
Messages with times,

126
00:03:51,592 --> 00:03:52,516
on the screen.

127
00:03:52,516 --> 00:03:54,891
Because they went to standard error,

128
00:03:54,891 --> 00:03:58,585
you can capture them without touching the normal output.

129
00:03:58,585 --> 00:04:00,102
Type: bash log dot S H,

130
00:04:00,102 --> 00:04:00,498
space,

131
00:04:00,498 --> 00:04:01,620
two greater than,

132
00:04:01,620 --> 00:04:02,411
run dot log.

133
00:04:02,961 --> 00:04:08,359
Silent, because the log went into the file. Look inside. Type: cat run dot log.

134
00:04:08,909 --> 00:04:10,253
A timestamped record.

135
00:04:10,253 --> 00:04:11,789
Results on standard out,

136
00:04:11,789 --> 00:04:13,517
and logs on standard error,

137
00:04:13,517 --> 00:04:15,565
so each can go to its own place.

138
00:04:17,615 --> 00:04:19,439
Now put it all together,

139
00:04:19,439 --> 00:04:20,883
in one safe script.

140
00:04:20,883 --> 00:04:21,796
Strict mode,

141
00:04:21,796 --> 00:04:23,088
a temporary file,

142
00:04:23,088 --> 00:04:23,620
a trap,

143
00:04:23,620 --> 00:04:24,912
and an exit code.

144
00:04:24,912 --> 00:04:26,660
Type: cat safe dot S H.

145
00:04:27,210 --> 00:04:29,049
Notice or true after grep.

146
00:04:29,049 --> 00:04:30,818
When grep finds no match,

147
00:04:30,818 --> 00:04:32,091
it exits with one,

148
00:04:32,091 --> 00:04:34,778
and strict mode would stop the script.

149
00:04:34,778 --> 00:04:36,900
Or true says: that is allowed.

150
00:04:36,900 --> 00:04:37,395
Run it.

151
00:04:37,395 --> 00:04:39,093
Type: bash safe dot S H.

152
00:04:39,643 --> 00:04:40,345
Two errors.

153
00:04:40,345 --> 00:04:42,322
The script then exits with one,

154
00:04:42,322 --> 00:04:43,534
to tell the caller.

155
00:04:43,534 --> 00:04:44,108
Check it.

156
00:04:44,108 --> 00:04:44,810
Type: echo,

157
00:04:44,810 --> 00:04:46,150
dollar question mark.

158
00:04:46,700 --> 00:04:47,129
Strict,

159
00:04:47,129 --> 00:04:47,497
clean,

160
00:04:47,497 --> 00:04:49,215
and honest about the result.

161
00:04:49,215 --> 00:04:52,038
That is a script you can run from a scheduler,

162
00:04:52,038 --> 00:04:52,652
and trust.

163
00:04:54,702 --> 00:04:56,025
Four myths to clear up.

164
00:04:56,575 --> 00:04:59,392
Myth one: exit code zero means failure.

165
00:04:59,392 --> 00:04:59,609
No.

166
00:04:59,609 --> 00:05:00,981
Zero means success.

167
00:05:00,981 --> 00:05:02,932
Anything else is a failure.

168
00:05:03,482 --> 00:05:06,436
Myth two: set dash E catches every error.

169
00:05:06,436 --> 00:05:06,652
No.

170
00:05:06,652 --> 00:05:08,598
Commands inside an if test,

171
00:05:08,598 --> 00:05:09,751
or before or or,

172
00:05:09,751 --> 00:05:10,543
are exempt.

173
00:05:11,093 --> 00:05:14,083
Myth three: a trap only runs on control C.

174
00:05:14,083 --> 00:05:14,297
No.

175
00:05:14,297 --> 00:05:17,429
A trap on EXIT runs however the script ends.

176
00:05:17,979 --> 00:05:20,698
Myth four: logs belong on standard out.

177
00:05:20,698 --> 00:05:20,907
No.

178
00:05:20,907 --> 00:05:22,860
Put results on standard out,

179
00:05:22,860 --> 00:05:24,742
and logs on standard error.

180
00:05:25,292 --> 00:05:28,215
Exit codes. Strict mode. Traps. Logs.

181
00:05:30,265 --> 00:05:34,830
Here is how you check your own understanding. Six points, and you need all six.

182
00:05:35,380 --> 00:05:40,074
Read an exit code with dollar question mark, and know that zero means success.

183
00:05:40,624 --> 00:05:43,397
Return your own exit code with the exit command.

184
00:05:43,947 --> 00:05:47,467
Use set dash E U O pipe fail, and explain each option.

185
00:05:48,017 --> 00:05:49,660
Clean up with a trap on EXIT.

186
00:05:50,210 --> 00:05:52,962
Write timestamped log lines to standard error.

187
00:05:53,512 --> 00:05:55,730
Allow an expected failure with or true.

188
00:05:56,280 --> 00:05:57,902
To pass, you need all six.

189
00:05:59,952 --> 00:06:04,752
Your turn, in your own Ubuntu virtual machine. Type every command yourself.

190
00:06:05,302 --> 00:06:09,697
One: run a command that works and one that fails, and print each exit code.

191
00:06:10,247 --> 00:06:14,305
Two: write a script that exits with one when a file contains a word,

192
00:06:14,305 --> 00:06:15,857
and zero when it does not.

193
00:06:16,407 --> 00:06:21,421
Three: add set dash E U O pipe fail, and prove that it stops at the first failure.

194
00:06:21,971 --> 00:06:27,112
Four: add a trap on EXIT that removes a temporary file, and check that none is left.

195
00:06:27,662 --> 00:06:31,589
Five: add a log function that writes timestamps to standard error,

196
00:06:31,589 --> 00:06:33,017
and save them to a file.

197
00:06:33,567 --> 00:06:38,281
Six: run grep on a file with no match, and make the script survive with or true.

198
00:06:38,831 --> 00:06:42,138
Mark yourself on the rubric. Pause the video, and do it now.

199
00:06:57,688 --> 00:06:58,563
Quick check.

200
00:06:59,113 --> 00:07:00,883
Which exit code means success?

201
00:07:01,433 --> 00:07:02,905
What does set dash U do?

202
00:07:03,455 --> 00:07:05,055
When does a trap on EXIT run?

203
00:07:05,605 --> 00:07:07,269
Where should log messages go?

204
00:07:07,819 --> 00:07:13,558
Objective covered: MC05-O06.

205
00:07:14,108 --> 00:07:16,177
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

206
00:07:26,727 --> 00:07:31,421
Next lesson: secrets, permissions, input validation and injection risks.

207
00:07:31,971 --> 00:07:35,299
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss it.
